france has historically not been shy  about territorial expansion be it in the  days of napoleon when it grew to this or  overseas where france built a large  colonial empire and french leadership  were by and large used to getting most  of the territorial demands that they  made of their defeated foes with a very  notable exception the sarland here which  despite a lot of effort from france  evaded their attempts to annex it but  how come why wasn't france ever able to  annex the sarland permanently so to  begin the sarland was briefly a part of  france during napoleon napoleon's years  of frolicking across europe but at that  point the lands held no special value  and they were simply the land west of  the rhine that napoleon wanted to make a  more defensible frontier after he was  dealt with france's borders returned to  this and later in that same century an  important discovery was made in the  sarland coal which was not only  important for heating and power but also  was fundamental in the production of  steel which was needed to produce  weapons in the late 19th century these  needs left france in a bind france had  very little coal of its own within its  own borders or even across its empire  and as such it needed to import it and  it did this from germany who could  switch off that supply at any moment and  thus for france to be secure it needed  its own large source of coal and its  leaders rying up the sarlan to be that  source as you'll know world war one saw  france and germany come to blows and  after germany's defeat the french wanted  the tsar land for their own the problem  was that simply grabbing your neighbor's  lands and making them your own was now  frowned upon by britain and the united  states who are pushing for things like  self-determination in europe that is not  the rest of the world nobody asked them  since the sarland was populated by  germans the french opted not to grab it  for their own but to do something a bit  more subtle and clever it was broken off  of germany and made into a league of  nations mandate for 15 years and at the  end of such time there would be a  referendum to decide on whether it would  return to germany stay independent or  join france and the french thought that  given germany's issues it was likely  that the people of starland would  overwhelmingly vote to join france but  fun fact no this may surprise you but  there was a second world war which  germany sticking with tradition lost as  well and the french still in need of  that sweet sweet coal figured that  they'd give grabbing the sarland one  more go when germany was divided into  occupation zones the french broke the  sarland off of germany again but this  time it was made a french protectorate  this annoyed britain and america but  france didn't care because they kept  getting invaded by germany so as far as  they were concerned this was fair  compensation by 1949 the sarland was  deeply linked with the french economy  and the living standards that the people  there had already recovered well ahead  of the rest of europe and it was clear  to everybody concerned that the sarland  was well on its way to becoming a part  of france proper so what stopped this it  was the unification of the allied  occupation zones into west germany the  cold war meant that the west needed  germany on their side and that germany  wanted the sarland back whereas france  wanted security and so its leaders  pushed for the creation of the european  coal and steel community which would  make whoever owned the sarland  irrelevant since france would be  guaranteed access to its coal by which  point the french were much less  interested in having the sarland for  themselves and were pushing for a  unified european approach to policy  something which required germany on side  that said the french gave the people of  the sarland a vote in 1955 on remaining  independent which the majority said no  to and two years after this the sarland  was reincorporated into germany where it  remains today i hope you enjoyed this  episode and thank you for watching with  a special thanks to my patrons james  bissonette kelly moneymaker mr wolf sky  chappelle jerry lamdin yn hockey marvin  cassell jordan longley gareth turner rod  d martin bugley wiggly captain cydog  spencer lightfoot corso wolfe gustav  swan matthew shipley winston caywood  robert wetzel aaron the white marcus  asner maggie paxkowski anthony beckett  alex schwinn mo the mukwopa coppertone  spinning three plates ben evanson  charles the first adam stalter and  scottish trekkie
 

